# ─── AR CI macOS Runner Configuration ──────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to .env, fill in the values, and install:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   $EDITOR .env
#   tools/bin/fleet macos install --store-from michael@mac-studio

# GitHub personal access token with 'repo' and 'admin:org' scopes
# (or fine-grained token with Administration read/write + Actions read)
GITHUB_PAT=ghp_your_token_here

# Registration scope: "repo" (default) registers against a single repository;
# "org" registers at the organization level so every repository in the org can
# share this runner (subject to the runner group's repository-access policy in
# Org Settings -> Actions -> Runner groups). Use "org" to share Macs between
# common and ringsdesktop.
# RUNNER_SCOPE=org

# GitHub owner (org or user). Always required.
GITHUB_OWNER=almostrealism

# Repository name. Required only when RUNNER_SCOPE=repo; ignored for org scope.
GITHUB_REPO=common

# Runner installation directory (default: ~<runner account>/actions-runner,
# or actions-runner-NAME for `fleet macos install --instance NAME`)
#
# Use an ABSOLUTE path. `fleet macos install` resolves `~` to the runner
# account's home, but runner.sh started by hand expands it to the home of
# whoever runs it — so the same .env can resolve to a different directory
# per user, silently.
# RUNNER_DIR=/Users/worker/actions-runner

# PATH the LaunchDaemon gives runner.sh and every job (default: the runner
# account's ~/.local/bin, Homebrew, Homebrew's keg-only openjdk@17, and the
# system directories). launchd starts a daemon with almost no PATH, so set
# this if java, mvn, jq or git live somewhere else for the runner account.
# `fleet macos install` checks they resolve on it before installing.
# RUNNER_PATH=/Users/worker/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/opt/openjdk@17/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin

# Runner name (default: $(hostname)-macos)
# RUNNER_NAME=my-mac-runner

# Runner group (default: Default)
# RUNNER_GROUP=Default

# Labels this runner advertises (default: self-hosted,macos,ar-ci)
#
# A job runs on any runner whose labels COVER the job's `runs-on` list, so what
# a runner refuses is decided by the labels it LEAVES OUT. Keep the default to
# serve the normal test lane.
#
# For a runner dedicated to deployment and nothing else, drop ar-ci and add
# ar-deploy. Without ar-ci it cannot match `[self-hosted, macos, ar-ci]`, so it
# takes the deploy job only:
# RUNNER_LABELS=self-hosted,macos,ar-deploy
#
# Do NOT instead add ar-deploy to an ar-ci runner — that runner would then
# cover both and take test jobs as well.
#
# For the runner that installs the native macOS FlowTree agent, use
# ar-deploy-agent instead of ar-deploy. This runner MUST be started as the
# account the agent runs as (the `worker` service account), which is not the
# account that administers Docker for the ar-deploy runner — the job writes
# the agent's install directory in that account's home and restarts the
# agent's process, and only the owner can do either. It does not need any
# launchd privilege: the agent is a LaunchDaemon an administrator registers
# once. See README, "Deploying the native macOS agent":
# RUNNER_LABELS=self-hosted,macos,ar-deploy-agent
#
# Running more than one role on a machine means one wrapper per role, each
# needing its own RUNNER_NAME and RUNNER_DIR (see README, "Dedicating a
# Runner to One Job").
# RUNNER_LABELS=self-hosted,macos,ar-ci

# Working directory for job execution (default: ~/actions-runner/_work)
# RUNNER_WORKDIR=/path/to/workdir

# Maximum CPUs the runner job is allowed to use (default: no limit).
# Requires cpulimit (brew install cpulimit). Ignored if cpulimit is
# not installed. Set based on the machine's capability — e.g. 4 on a
# shared Mac Mini, 8 on a dedicated Mac Studio.
# RUNNER_CPU_LIMIT=4
